**Verdict: refuted for arbitrary descent DAGs.** A shared-eliminator DAG can produce independent base relations over both \(\mathbb F_2\) and \(\mathbb Z\) with zero fresh smoothness revelations. The construction has exponent \(1\). The \(1.2114/1.5263\) floors and the \(\alpha\ge 1/3\) barrier remain open for arbitrary DAGs under H. [Full proof and cost ledger](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L3c/lemma3.txt:1)

**Proved.** For a row matrix \(M\) with large-column block \(M_L\), the extracted base rank is exactly
\[
r=\operatorname{rank}M-\operatorname{rank}M_L.
\]
Take ranks over \(\mathbb Q\) for the integer lattice. A row introducing an unseen odd large column contributes **zero** new base rank at that step. Base rank rises by one precisely when the full row is independent and its large part lies in the earlier large span. Over \(\mathbb F_2\), stripping all exposed large primes then leaves a smooth part times a square. Recognizing that square afterward cannot retroactively supply a fresh probe. [Rank and credit proof](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L3c/lemma3.txt:5)

**Priced counterexample.** Take \(m\) base primes \(b_i\), one large prime \(q\), and generators \(q^3b_i\bmod N\). Enumerate their cosets modulo \(\langle q\rangle\). Each table lookup certifies an equality between already-factored words. All rows share the single external column \(q\). Eliminating it gives integer rank \(m\) and binary rank at least \(m-\omega(N)\), where \(\omega(N)\) counts distinct prime factors. Every fully stripped cofactor is \(1\). For a semiprime and \(B=L_N[\eta,c]\), \(0<\eta<1\), the table contains \(N^{1-o(1)}\) distinct residues already from products of base primes below \(N\). Its total cost is \(N^{1+o(1)}\). [Construction and pricing](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L3c/lemma3.txt:18)

**Measured.** At \(N=2773\), the DAG gave integer rank **10**, binary rank **8**, and a certified gcd of **47**. A primitive two-map form also realized the \(q^3b_i/q\) star exactly. Stripping \(q^3\) left the five prescribed \(b_i\), yielding rank five and zero square dependencies. In a separate check, every actual large-prime strip left **the same integer 11**. Five prime ideals above 11 were identified before evaluation and yielded four independent base rows. [Exact results](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L3c/measured.txt:1) · [Primitive-form construction](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L3c/lemma3.txt:30)

**Next step.** Bound how much rank from cached factors, recognized squares, and modular equalities can contribute to a *fruitful certificate* within \(L_N[1/3,c]\) work. That is the missing term in the rank bound. At simple projective roots, the arithmetic part of DAG-POOL already extends to stripping the full prime power. [Restricted extension and remaining lemma](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/ch_L3c/lemma3.txt:40)